window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-668fd22585595004"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"sandbox\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">沙盒</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 后追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该文档的 Markdown 版本。</p> <p><strong>适用界面：app,cli,ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>沙盒提供了一道边界，让智能体可以自主执行任务，而不会因此获得对电脑的不受限制访问权限。当本地对话在 <strong>ChatGPT 桌面应用</strong>、<strong>Codex CLI</strong> 或 <strong>IDE 扩展</strong>中运行命令时，这些命令会在受限环境中运行，而不是默认拥有完整访问权。</p> <p>这个环境会规定智能体可以自行执行哪些操作，例如可修改哪些文件，以及命令能否访问网络。任务如果始终处于这些边界之内，智能体便可以继续工作，不必停下来请求确认；若需要越过边界，则会由审批流程接手。</p> <p>沙盒和审批是两种不同但相互配合的控制。沙盒定义技术边界；审批策略决定智能体在越过边界之前何时必须暂停并询问。</p> <p><a id=\"what-the-sandbox-does\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">沙盒的作用</h2> <p>沙盒适用于启动的命令，不只适用于内置文件操作。如果智能体运行 <code>git</code>、软件包管理器或测试运行器等工具，这些命令也会继承相同的沙盒边界。</p> <p>Codex 在不同操作系统上使用平台原生的强制执行机制。macOS、Linux、WSL2 和原生 Windows 的实现方式各不相同，但目标一致：为智能体提供一个范围明确、受到限制的工作环境，让日常任务可以在清楚的边界内自主运行。</p> <p><a id=\"why-it-matters\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">为什么沙盒很重要</h2> <p>沙盒可以减少频繁审批带来的疲劳。你预先批准边界后，智能体就能在其中读取文件、编辑内容并运行常规项目命令，而不必为每个低风险命令都请求确认。</p> <p>沙盒也让智能体工作的信任模型更清楚。你信任的不只是智能体的意图，也包括它在强制执行的限制内运行。这样更容易让智能体自主工作，同时清楚知道它何时会停下来寻求帮助。</p> <p><a id=\"getting-started\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">开始使用</h2> <p>默认权限模式会自动启用沙盒。</p> <p><a id=\"prerequisites\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_5\">前置条件</h3> <p>在 <strong>macOS</strong> 上，沙盒使用内置的 Seatbelt 框架，开箱即用。</p> <p>在 <strong>Windows</strong> 上，通过 PowerShell 运行时，Codex 使用原生的 <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2?anchor=windows-sandbox\">Windows 沙盒</a>；在 WSL2 中运行时，则使用 Linux 沙盒实现。</p> <p>在 <strong>Linux 和 WSL2</strong> 上，请先使用软件包管理器安装 <code>bubblewrap</code>：</p> <p><strong>codex-sandboxing-prerequisites</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo apt install bubblewrap\n</code></pre> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo dnf install bubblewrap\n</code></pre> <p>Codex 会使用在 <code>PATH</code> 中找到的第一个 <code>bwrap</code> 可执行文件。如果找不到 <code>bwrap</code>，Codex 会退回到随程序提供的辅助工具，但该工具要求系统支持创建非特权用户命名空间。安装提供 <code>bwrap</code> 的发行版软件包，可以让此设置更可靠。</p> <p>如果缺少 <code>bwrap</code>，或辅助工具无法创建所需用户命名空间，Codex 会在启动时显示警告。在限制此 AppArmor 设置的发行版上，建议加载 <code>bwrap</code> AppArmor 配置文件，让 <code>bwrap</code> 可以正常工作，而不必全局关闭限制。</p> <p><strong>Ubuntu AppArmor 说明：</strong> 在 Ubuntu 25.04 上，从 Ubuntu 软件包仓库安装 <code>bubblewrap</code> 后通常无需额外设置 AppArmor。<code>bwrap-userns-restrict</code> 配置文件随 <code>apparmor</code> 软件包提供，路径为 <code>/etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict</code>。</p> <p>在 Ubuntu 24.04 上，即使安装了 <code>bubblewrap</code>，Codex 仍可能提示无法创建所需用户命名空间。请复制并加载额外配置文件：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo apt update\nsudo apt install apparmor-profiles apparmor-utils\nsudo install -m 0644 \\\n  /usr/share/apparmor/extra-profiles/bwrap-userns-restrict \\\n  /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict\nsudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict\n</code></pre> <p><code>apparmor_parser -r</code> 会将配置文件加载到内核，无需重启。你也可以重新加载所有 AppArmor 配置文件：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo systemctl reload apparmor.service\n</code></pre> <p>如果没有该配置文件，或问题仍未解决，可以关闭 AppArmor 对非特权用户命名空间的限制：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo sysctl -w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0\n</code></pre> <blockquote> <p><strong>适用界面：app,web</strong></p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"synced-chatgpt-work\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"work-cloud\">Work Cloud 本地计算机访问</h4> <p>启用 Work Cloud 本地计算机访问的任务由 OpenAI 云端协调。沙盒限制取决于每个步骤的运行位置：</p> <ul> <li><strong>云端执行：</strong> Work 云容器使用现有 Work Cloud 策略。桌面浏览器的网站规则不会自动应用到云端浏览器。</li> <li><strong>本地执行：</strong> 受支持的本地执行要求约束连接电脑上的步骤。</li> </ul> <p>对于通过本地访问和 dots 使用 Work 的情形，启用托管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略适用于共享云协调器。Work 云容器和 dots 云电脑使用各自的执行配置与要求。请分别测试本地和云端执行，确认每种环境适用哪些控制项。一种环境的策略不会授予对另一种环境的访问权。</p> <p>在单个策略中，优先级从高到低依次为：操作系统专属环境覆盖项 → 全操作系统环境覆盖项 → Global。在本地执行中，MDM 和旧版托管设备要求优先于 Agent Security；Agent Security 又优先于设备的系统要求文件。</p> <p>有关字段专属的合并规则和运行时限制，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>；有关实际生效的文件和网络限制，请查看 <a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801\">Work 本地安全</a>与 <a href=\"#/article/a-eea2edbcef587cb2\">Work 云端安全</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"how-permissions-work\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">权限如何运作</h2> <blockquote> <p><strong>适用界面：app,cli,ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>使用对应界面的权限控制项，调整 Codex 处理本地操作的方式。</p> <p>审批决定 Codex 何时需要在操作前暂停；沙盒决定命令可访问哪些文件和网络资源。如果审批选项提供不同范围（例如仅批准一次或批准整个会话），请选择足以继续任务的最小范围。默认保持项目边界；需要在不同仓库中工作时，请使用独立项目或 worktree，而不是扩大到互不相关的代码库。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>适用界面：web</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>ChatGPT Work 会在托管的隔离环境中运行代码和 Shell 命令。工作区策略和工具专属控制项决定可使用哪些能力。如果有此设置，请通过 <strong>Settings（设置）&gt; Data controls（数据控制）&gt; Work network access（Work 网络访问）</strong> 管理代码和 Shell 命令的网络访问。打开 <strong>Allow public internet access（允许访问公共互联网）</strong> 后，这些命令可以访问公共互联网；关闭后则只能访问托管允许列表中的必需主机名。</p> <p>网页搜索、插件和远程浏览器分别受独立控制。当前代码或 Shell 运行结束且 Work 刷新执行环境后，更改才会生效。ChatGPT 网页版不提供本地 Codex 沙盒或审批模式选择器。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>适用界面：app</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中，使用输入框下方的权限控制项。根据你的配置，菜单可能包括 <strong>Ask for approval</strong>、适用于符合条件审批请求的 <strong>Approve for me</strong>、<strong>Full access</strong>，以及命名权限配置文件或自定义权限配置文件。</p> <p class=\"notice\">权限模式交互演示需要联网查看官方页面；示例不会更改你的实际权限。适用界面：命令行。</p> <p>在 CLI 中输入 <a href=\"#/article/a-820e54e1b31b71d3?sectionMissing=cli-update-permissions-with-permissions\" title=\"旧来源章节未在本地快照中定位，打开该文章；可通过官方来源查看当前章节。\"><code>/permissions</code>（官网章节，需联网）</a> <a href=\"#/article/a-820e54e1b31b71d3\">本地页面（未识别该章节）</a>，打开权限选择器并更改当前使用的权限配置文件。</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>适用界面：ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>在 IDE 扩展中，使用输入框下方的权限控制项。根据你的配置，菜单可能包括 <strong>Ask for approval</strong>、适用于符合条件审批请求的 <strong>Approve for me</strong>、<strong>Full access</strong>，以及命名权限配置文件或自定义权限配置文件。</p> <blockquote> <p>图示：IDE 扩展中的 Codex 审批模式选择器</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure-defaults\"></a></p> <blockquote> <p><strong>适用界面：app,cli,ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"_7\">配置默认值</h2> <p>如果希望每次都从相同的行为开始，可以在 <code>config.toml</code> 中设置默认值。<a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76\">配置基础</a>介绍配置文件的工作方式；<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>列出 <code>sandbox_mode</code>、<code>approval_policy</code>、<code>approvals_reviewer</code> 和 <code>sandbox_workspace_write.writable_roots</code> 的准确键名。使用这些设置，可以决定智能体默认拥有多大自主权、可写入哪些目录、何时暂停请求审批，以及由谁审查符合条件的审批请求。</p> <p>常见沙盒模式大致如下：</p> <ul> <li><code>read-only</code>：智能体可以检查文件，但未经批准不能编辑文件或运行命令。</li> <li><code>workspace-write</code>：智能体可以读取文件、在工作区内编辑文件，并在此边界内运行常规本地命令。这是本地工作的默认低摩擦模式。</li> <li><code>danger-full-access</code>：智能体不受沙盒限制地运行。这会移除文件系统和网络边界，只应在你希望智能体拥有完整访问权时使用。</li> </ul> <p>常见审批策略如下：</p> <ul> <li><code>on-request</code>：智能体默认在沙盒内工作，需要越过边界时会请求批准。</li> <li><code>never</code>：智能体不会停下来请求审批。</li> </ul> <p>Codex 和 ChatGPT Work 已不再支持将 <code>untrusted</code> 作为可选审批策略。如果现有配置使用了此值，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=migrate-from-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">从已停用的 <code>untrusted</code> 审批策略迁移</a>。</p> <p>审批采用交互方式时，还可以通过 <code>approvals_reviewer</code> 选择审批人：</p> <ul> <li><code>user</code>：审批请求会显示给用户。这是默认值。</li> <li><code>auto_review</code>：符合条件的审批请求会交给审查智能体（请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">自动审查</a>）。</li> </ul> <p>完整访问模式需要同时设置 <code>sandbox_mode = \"danger-full-access\"</code> 和 <code>approval_policy = \"never\"</code>。相比之下，风险较低的本地自动化预设是 <code>sandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"</code> 加 <code>approval_policy = \"on-request\"</code>，或使用对应 CLI 参数 <code>--sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval on-request</code>。此时可以保留 <code>approvals_reviewer = \"user\"</code> 进行人工审批，或设置为 <code>approvals_reviewer = \"auto_review\"</code> 以自动审查审批请求。</p> <p>如果需要让智能体在多个目录中工作，可以使用可写入根目录扩展可修改范围，而不必完全移除沙盒。如果需要更宽或更窄的信任边界，应调整默认沙盒模式和审批策略，而不是依赖一次性的例外。</p> <p>对于只需特定例外的工作流，请使用<a href=\"#/article/a-6c7aa55b479f9cdd\">规则</a>。规则可以允许、提示或禁止沙盒外的命令前缀，通常比大范围扩大访问权更合适。IDE 专属设置入口请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-7969ca0198aaf47f\">Codex IDE 扩展设置</a>。</p> <p>如果功能可用，自动审查不会改变沙盒边界；它只是审批请求的一种 <code>approvals_reviewer</code> 选项，例如审查沙盒权限升级、被阻止的网络访问或仍需批准的有副作用工具调用。沙盒内本来就已允许的操作会直接运行，不需要额外审查。有关审查流程、触发类型、拒绝语义和配置详情，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">自动审查</a>。</p> <p>平台专属详情请参阅相应文档。有关原生 Windows 的设置、行为和故障排查，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2\">Windows</a>。有关管理员要求以及组织对沙盒与审批的限制，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">智能体审批与安全</a>。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"sandbox\">Sandbox</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> <p><strong>Surface: app,cli,ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>The sandbox is the boundary that lets the agent act autonomously without giving it unrestricted access to your machine. When a local chat runs commands in the <strong>ChatGPT desktop app</strong>, <strong>Codex CLI</strong>, or <strong>IDE extension</strong>, those commands run inside a constrained environment instead of running with full access by default.</p> <p>That environment defines what the agent can do on its own, such as which files it can modify and whether commands can use the network. When a task stays inside those boundaries, the agent can keep moving without stopping for confirmation. When it needs to go beyond them, the approval flow takes over.</p> <p>Sandboxing and approvals are different controls that work together. The sandbox defines technical boundaries. The approval policy decides when the agent must stop and ask before crossing them.</p> <h2 id=\"what-the-sandbox-does\">What the sandbox does</h2> <p>The sandbox applies to spawned commands, not just to built-in file operations. If the agent runs tools like <code>git</code>, package managers, or test runners, those commands inherit the same sandbox boundaries.</p> <p>Codex uses platform-native enforcement on each OS. The implementation differs between macOS, Linux, WSL2, and native Windows, but the idea is the same across surfaces: give the agent a bounded place to work so routine tasks can run autonomously inside clear limits.</p> <h2 id=\"why-it-matters\">Why it matters</h2> <p>The sandbox reduces approval fatigue. Instead of asking you to confirm every low-risk command, the agent can read files, make edits, and run routine project commands within the boundary you already approved.</p> <p>It also gives you a clearer trust model for agentic work. You aren't just trusting the agent's intentions; you are trusting that the agent is operating inside enforced limits. That makes it easier to let the agent work independently while still knowing when it will stop and ask for help.</p> <h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2> <p>The default permissions mode applies sandboxing automatically.</p> <h3 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites</h3> <p>On <strong>macOS</strong>, sandboxing works out of the box using the built-in Seatbelt framework.</p> <p>On <strong>Windows</strong>, Codex uses the native <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2?anchor=windows-sandbox\">Windows sandbox</a> when you run in PowerShell and the Linux sandbox implementation when you run in WSL2.</p> <p>On <strong>Linux and WSL2</strong>, install <code>bubblewrap</code> with your package manager first:</p> <p><strong>codex-sandboxing-prerequisites</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo apt install bubblewrap\n</code></pre> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo dnf install bubblewrap\n</code></pre> <p>Codex uses the first <code>bwrap</code> executable it finds on <code>PATH</code>. If no <code>bwrap</code> executable is available, Codex falls back to a bundled helper, but that helper requires support for unprivileged user namespace creation. Installing the distribution package that provides <code>bwrap</code> keeps this setup reliable.</p> <p>Codex surfaces a startup warning when <code>bwrap</code> is missing or when the helper can't create the needed user namespace. On distributions that restrict this AppArmor setting, prefer loading the <code>bwrap</code> AppArmor profile so <code>bwrap</code> can keep working without disabling the restriction globally.</p> <p><strong>Ubuntu AppArmor note:</strong> On Ubuntu 25.04, installing <code>bubblewrap</code> from Ubuntu's package repository should work without extra AppArmor setup. The <code>bwrap-userns-restrict</code> profile ships in the <code>apparmor</code> package at <code>/etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict</code>.</p> <p>On Ubuntu 24.04, Codex may still warn that it can't create the needed user namespace after <code>bubblewrap</code> is installed. Copy and load the extra profile:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo apt update\nsudo apt install apparmor-profiles apparmor-utils\nsudo install -m 0644 \\\n  /usr/share/apparmor/extra-profiles/bwrap-userns-restrict \\\n  /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict\nsudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict\n</code></pre> <p><code>apparmor_parser -r</code> loads the profile into the kernel without a reboot. You can also reload all AppArmor profiles:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo systemctl reload apparmor.service\n</code></pre> <p>If that profile is unavailable or does not resolve the issue, you can disable the AppArmor unprivileged user namespace restriction with:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">sudo sysctl -w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0\n</code></pre> <blockquote> <p><strong>Surface: app,web</strong></p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"synced-chatgpt-work\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\">Local computer access with Work Cloud</h4> <p>For tasks using Local computer access with Work Cloud, OpenAI's cloud coordinates the conversation. Sandbox restrictions depend on where each step runs:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Cloud execution:</strong> Work cloud containers use existing Work Cloud policies. Desktop browser site rules do not automatically apply to cloud browsers.</li> <li><strong>Local execution:</strong> Supported local execution requirements govern steps on a connected computer.</li> </ul> <p>For Work with local access and dots, supported Global policy applies to the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled. Work cloud containers and dots cloud computers use their own execution configuration and requirements. Test local and cloud execution separately to confirm which controls apply in each environment. A policy for one environment doesn't grant access to another.</p> <p>Within a policy, the order from highest to lowest is OS-specific environment override → all-OS environment override → Global. A higher-priority policy wins even when a lower-priority policy is more specific. For local execution, MDM and legacy managed-device requirements take precedence over Agent Security, which takes precedence over the device's system requirements file.</p> <p>Review <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> for field-specific merge rules and runtime limits, and check the effective file and network restrictions in <a href=\"#/article/a-54e559ed4245e801\">Work local security</a> and <a href=\"#/article/a-eea2edbcef587cb2\">Work cloud security</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"how-permissions-work\">How permissions work</h2> <blockquote> <p><strong>Surface: app,cli,ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>Use the permissions control for your surface to change how Codex handles local actions.</p> <p>Approvals determine when Codex pauses before an action, while the sandbox determines which files and network resources commands can access. When an approval offers different scopes, such as approving once or for the session, choose the narrowest scope that lets the task continue. Keep the project boundary as the default; use separate projects or worktrees instead of broadening access across unrelated repositories.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Surface: web</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>ChatGPT Work runs code and shell commands in a managed, isolated environment. Workspace policy and tool-specific controls determine which capabilities are available. When the setting is available, use <strong>Settings &gt; Data controls &gt; Work network access</strong> to manage network access for code and shell commands. Turn on <strong>Allow public internet access</strong> to let those commands reach the public internet. When it's off, commands can reach only required hostnames from a managed allowlist.</p> <p>Web search, plugins, and the remote browser have separate controls. Changes take effect after the current code or shell run finishes and Work refreshes its execution environment. ChatGPT web doesn't expose the local Codex sandbox or approval-mode selector.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Surface: app</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>In the ChatGPT desktop app, use the permissions control beneath the composer. Depending on your configuration, the menu can include <strong>Ask for approval</strong>, <strong>Approve for me</strong> for eligible approval requests, <strong>Full access</strong>, and named or custom permissions profiles.</p> <p class=\"notice\">Open the official page online for the permission-mode demo. Examples do not change your actual permissions. Surface: CLI.</p> <p>In the CLI, enter <a href=\"#/article/a-820e54e1b31b71d3?sectionMissing=cli-update-permissions-with-permissions\" title=\"旧来源章节未在本地快照中定位，打开该文章；可通过官方来源查看当前章节。\"><code>/permissions</code> (official section, online)</a> <a href=\"#/article/a-820e54e1b31b71d3\">Local page (section not recognized)</a> to open the permissions picker and change the active permissions profile.</p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Surface: ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <p>In the IDE extension, use the permissions control beneath the composer. Depending on your configuration, the menu can include <strong>Ask for approval</strong>, <strong>Approve for me</strong> for eligible approval requests, <strong>Full access</strong>, and named or custom permissions profiles.</p> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Codex approval mode selector in the IDE extension</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"configure-defaults\"></a></p> <blockquote> <p><strong>Surface: app,cli,ide</strong></p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"configure-defaults\">Configure defaults</h2> <p>To start with the same behavior every time, set defaults in <code>config.toml</code>. <a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76\">Config basics</a> explains how it works, and the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration reference</a> documents the exact keys for <code>sandbox_mode</code>, <code>approval_policy</code>, <code>approvals_reviewer</code>, and <code>sandbox_workspace_write.writable_roots</code>. Use those settings to decide how much autonomy the agent gets by default, which directories it can write to, when it should pause for approval, and who reviews eligible approval requests.</p> <p>At a high level, the common sandbox modes are:</p> <ul> <li><code>read-only</code>: The agent can inspect files, but it can't edit files or run commands without approval.</li> <li><code>workspace-write</code>: The agent can read files, edit within the workspace, and run routine local commands inside that boundary. This is the default low-friction mode for local work.</li> <li><code>danger-full-access</code>: The agent runs without sandbox restrictions. This removes the filesystem and network boundaries and should be used only when you want the agent to act with full access.</li> </ul> <p>The common approval policies are:</p> <ul> <li><code>on-request</code>: The agent works inside the sandbox by default and asks when it needs to go beyond that boundary.</li> <li><code>never</code>: The agent doesn't stop for approval prompts.</li> </ul> <p>Codex and ChatGPT Work no longer support <code>untrusted</code> as a selectable approval policy. If an existing configuration uses that value, see <a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=migrate-from-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">Migrate from the retired <code>untrusted</code> approval policy</a>.</p> <p>When approvals are interactive, you can also choose who reviews them with <code>approvals_reviewer</code>:</p> <ul> <li><code>user</code>: approval prompts surface to the user. This is the default.</li> <li><code>auto_review</code>: eligible approval prompts go to a reviewer agent (see <a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">automatic review</a>).</li> </ul> <p>Full access means using <code>sandbox_mode = \"danger-full-access\"</code> together with <code>approval_policy = \"never\"</code>. By contrast, the lower-risk local automation preset is <code>sandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"</code> together with <code>approval_policy = \"on-request\"</code>, or the matching CLI flags <code>--sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval on-request</code>. You can then keep <code>approvals_reviewer = \"user\"</code> for manual approvals or set <code>approvals_reviewer = \"auto_review\"</code> for automatic approval review.</p> <p>If you need the agent to work across more than one directory, writable roots let you extend the places it can modify without removing the sandbox entirely. If you need a broader or narrower trust boundary, adjust the default sandbox mode and approval policy instead of relying on one-off exceptions.</p> <p>When a workflow needs a specific exception, use <a href=\"#/article/a-6c7aa55b479f9cdd\">rules</a>. Rules let you allow, prompt, or forbid command prefixes outside the sandbox, which is often a better fit than broadly expanding access. For IDE-specific settings entry points, see <a href=\"#/article/a-7969ca0198aaf47f\">Codex IDE extension settings</a>.</p> <p>Automatic review, when available, doesn't change the sandbox boundary. It's one possible <code>approvals_reviewer</code> for approval requests at that boundary, such as sandbox escalations, blocked network access, or side-effecting tool calls that still need approval. Actions already allowed inside the sandbox run without extra review. For the reviewer lifecycle, trigger types, denial semantics, and configuration details, see <a href=\"#/article/a-f464374c0d895ab1\">automatic review</a>.</p> <p>Platform details live in the platform-specific docs. For native Windows setup, behavior, and troubleshooting, see <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2\">Windows</a>. For admin requirements and organization-level constraints on sandboxing and approvals, see <a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">Agent approvals &amp; security</a>.</p>"};
